    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d105">

                <pb facs="0165" n="73" sameAs="frus:frus1943CairoTehran/pg73"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt
                    Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The President to the
                        Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>)<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d105-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-11-08">8 November 1943</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>To Ambassador Winant from the
                    President.</p>
                <p>Your message of 8 November. I think it would be fine.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Roosevelt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d105-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d105-f1-content">
                <p>Sent to the United States Naval Attaché, London, via Navy channels. After
                    receiving this telegram, Winant cabled
                        Hopkins: “Would you be good enough to
                    explain my journey to Ed Stettinius.
                    Telegrams to the Department will continue to carry my name. …” Hopkins forwarded the message to Stettinius on November 11, 1943 (123 Winant, John G./250).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
